---
description: Warp operating constraints. Gates, locks, beam file, and merge policy. No per-person cap.
alwaysApply: false
---

# Warp operating rules

- `.warp/beam.json` is the source of truth. Update it only through `scripts/beam.py` or `scripts/scan.py`.
- Do not dispatch unless `runState` is `running`.
- Do not start a ticket until every dep is merged and every blocking upstream gate is green.
- Do not run two active tickets whose lock paths overlap.
- The orchestrator is the only merger. It dispatches, merges, and tracks, and it writes no ticket product code. Shuttles and Reed do not merge and do not push to the base branch.
- `maxAgents` caps concurrent Shuttle slots. A project may set 18. Do not hardcode a cap. `maxInProgress` (default 20) caps tickets that have started and are not merged or parked. At that cap, take nothing new from the ready queue. It does not stop fixes, rebases, CI reruns, Bugbot re-requests, or merges. There are two caps. `maxAgents` is how many agents run at once: every Shuttle, whatever its step, here or on its own VM. `maxInProgress` is how many tickets are open at once. A fix, a rebase, or a rerun is an agent like any other and counts against `maxAgents`. It still starts when in progress is over `maxInProgress`, because its ticket is already open. Start and resume launch unfinished work first: fixes, then tickets whose Shuttle was halted or died, then new tickets. Parked tickets do not count. Do not park or cancel tickets just because the run is already over the cap.
- One ticket, one branch. `subagentVm` defaults to false. `runner: local` forces it false and forces `launch` to `worktree`, skips the cloud-environment start check, and logs one note. Start, `/warp-version`, and status print the effective values. `memoryCheck` defaults to false, so only `maxAgents` applies until `true` lets `free -g` lower it on this machine. On a cloud runner, `true` asks for a dedicated VM. The prompt says: run in your own cloud environment on a dedicated VM with its own clone and branch, not a git worktree on this machine. Start one subagent per ticket, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. The same hostname falls back to a worktree on this machine, with one warning per run. `/in-cloud` in the Agents Window is the manual fallback, one ticket per invocation. Cloud subagents use the MCP servers at cursor.com/agents, not this session. Start one subagent per ticket in its own worktree, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. `checkout.py launch` fetches origin and runs `git worktree add <worktreeRoot>/<id> -b warp/<id>-<jira> origin/main`. The prompt names the ticket, Jira key, locks, acceptance, branch, and absolute worktree path. The subagent works only inside that path, commits, pushes, and opens the pull request. It never merges, never touches the parent checkout, and never calls Jira or Slack. It writes `.warp/tickets/<id>/state.json` and `log.jsonl` into the parent checkout by absolute path. `checkout.py verify` raises `lock-escape` when the parent is dirty outside `.warp/` or the diff leaves the locks. Merge or park runs `git worktree remove` and `git worktree prune`. A failed result raises `worker-died`. `/warp-resume` reuses the surviving worktree and branch. Optional `launch: agent` prints an IMPLEMENT prompt for one new Agent. This plugin does not call a Cloud Agents API. Clone main so `.cursor` rules load, then create the branch. A beam file does not have to exist on the branch before that Agent starts. Never two tickets in one worktree. Never two workers on one ticket. The beam stores the agent id or the worktree path.
- Ready means every dependency and every `after` id is merged on the base branch, and no lock path overlaps a lock held by an in-flight ticket. Overlap is the same path or a parent folder. Use the ticket's full lock list. An open or green pull request is not merged.
- Dispatch at start and after every merge, failure, and freed slot. Starred and priority first, then lowest rank, until `maxAgents` are in flight. Do not hold a slot for a ticket that is not ready. Do not wait for a whole dependency level.
- Opening a pull request does not finish the ticket. The slot stays occupied until the GitHub or Bitbucket check rollup is green. Bugbot runs on the pull request after push, not inside the VM. Locks stay until merge or park. `checkCommand` empty means Bugbot and CI as configured. `make ci` is kept as the full check command. A red result is stored on `pr.check` and the ticket is sent back with the log (`send-back <id> fix` and a `start` line). Past `maxFixAttempts` (default 5) the ticket parks. The name alone is not a red check. The gate stays while that check is red. `appendOnlyPaths` is empty by default. A conflict there keeps both sides, every added line. Any other conflict is sent back. Do not hand-merge it. `mergeQueue` defaults to false. When it is true, or GitHub reports a merge queue, enqueue the pull request. A direct merge that branch protection rejects is not marked merged. `state_commit.py commit` commits the beam, journal, STATUS, and BOARD and strips tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs. `.warp/config.yaml` stays gitignored. On a cloud runner, `/warp-start` refuses when `transitionJiraIssue`, `addOrEditJiraIssueComment`, or `slack_send_message` is missing from the MCP allow list. `--force` starts anyway. A local runner does not block.
- The merge queue is serial: starred first, then lowest rank. Rebase, run the check, merge, delete the branch, dispatch. Sizes outside `autoMergeSizes` still wait for `/warp-proceed` or `warp:proceed`. Past `maxFixAttempts` (default 5) a red check parks the ticket, releases its locks, and leaves the branch and pull request open. A red base branch stops merging and a fix is dispatched ahead of every rank. Restart classifies merged, in flight, and pending from git.
- On start or resume, rebuild from git: merged on the base branch, in flight when a branch or pull request is open, otherwise pending. Done means every ticket is merged or explicitly parked, and the base branch is green.
- There is no per-person cap. `maxAgents` caps concurrent Shuttle slots. `maxInProgress` (default 20) caps started tickets that are not merged or parked and only holds new ready-queue launches. Fix Shuttles count against `maxAgents` like any other agent.
- A size in `autoMergeSizes` auto-merges after Bugbot and CI are green, including L and XL when they are listed. L and XL are not special. The default list is S, M, so those wait for a provider approval or `warp:proceed <id>` unless you add them. QA Ready is not set before Bugbot is clean.
- The git provider is `gitProvider` in `.warp/config.yaml` (`auto`, `github`, or `bitbucket`). `pushMerge: false` means local branches and a local merge, no push and no pull request.
- Coding model comes from `.warp/config.yaml`. Default `claude-sonnet-5-5-high` (Claude Sonnet 5.5 High). The slug must match the Cursor model picker.
- Jira, GitHub, Bitbucket, Slack, and Teams go through Cursor's connected MCP servers, or `gh` for GitHub when `ghCli` is true. If none is usable, fall back to local-only.
- When `beam.py set` prints `jira: MUST DO`, do every action in `.warp/jira-todo.json` in that turn, then `jira_sync.py record` and `record-comment`. A later failure goes to `.warp/outbox.md` and Herald and does not stop that ticket. The first claimed ticket of a run is different: if Jira says the issue was not found, or no real issue key can be resolved, release the claim back to `queued` and stop the run. `jiraTransition: false` does not stop the run.
- One agent is long-lived: the parent. The parent is the only agent that starts another agent, and the only agent that waits. A Shuttle does one step of one ticket and returns one line. The listener does one poll and returns one line. A worker never starts an agent, subscribes, sets a timer, loops, sleeps, or waits on a pull request, CI, Bugbot, Slack, or an approval. `docs/LIFECYCLE.md` is the rule.
- A Shuttle prompt comes only from `checkout.py launch`, which records the Shuttle in `.warp/agents.json` first and prints nothing (`spawn: closed <id>`, exit 2) while the run is paused, stopped, or finished, or for a merged or parked ticket. The parent passes a subagent only the lines below `prompt: pass every line below this one to the subagent, and nothing above it`. Every prompt carries `agent: <id>`, the worker contract, and the reap command. Every agent Warp starts is named `[warp:<instance>] <ticket> <step>` or `[warp:<instance>] listener`: `checkout.py launch` prints the `name:` line, and the parent uses it. Cursor has no tag field, so that name is the tag pause, stop, and `/warp-cleanup` match on. `<instance>` is six characters that name this Warp run, set once by `/warp-start` and kept on the beam. `/warp-status`, `/warp-version`, and every Slack and Teams header show it as `warp:<instance>`. Another Warp run, in this repo or another, has a different instance and is never touched.
- Every Warp agent runs the reap check first and reads the `reap:` line after each state it writes (`agents.py reap`, and the line `ticket_state.py append`, `beam.py heartbeat`, and `inbound.py poll` print). `reap: exit <reason>` means stop now and return `result: <id> stopped <reason>`. A Shuttle on its own VM passes `--remote` and reads the beam from origin's base branch.
- The listener is a Subagent of the parent, and it is one poll. Start one subagent per ticket in its own worktree, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. The listener shares the parent's session and checkout. It is not a separate Agent. The parent runs `orchestrator.py supervise`. `listener: poll` is followed by the prompt (`LISTEN once`): the parent starts exactly one `warp-listen` Subagent in the foreground with it. The listener runs `inbound.py poll`, reads Slack and Teams once from the `since` mark, posts acks, enqueues `warp:` commands, records the poll with `inbound.py polled` (`.warp/listener.json` gets `lastPollAt` and `reason`), and returns `listen: <count>`. It does not loop and it does not sleep. `listener: hold`, `listener: idle`, and `listener: none` do not start one. The next poll is due `pollSeconds` after the last one finished. A poll that never reports is issued again after `listenerStaleMinutes`. The listener does not implement tickets, does not merge, and does not write product code. The parent runs `inbound.py apply-pending` and patches the live beam. The listener does not start a ticket subagent.
- Each parent pass runs `inbound.py apply-pending` and supervise, reads `.warp/tickets/<id>/`, patches the live beam, runs `checkout.py launch`, starts one subagent in that worktree, pushes the beam, and ends with `orchestrator.py parent-exit --wait --session <session>`. That command is the one wait in a run. `parent: stay` means run the next pass, while any ticket is not merged or parked. `parent: exit` is when every ticket is merged or parked, or the run is paused or stopped. `parent: exit superseded` means a later start or resume owns the run. A launch refusal is not idle. One parent per run: `<session>` is the id `scan.py start` prints as `parent: session <id>`.
- `/warp-start` and `/warp-resume` are the same command. Either one sets the run running from paused, from stopped, or when it is already running, prints where it came from (`run: was paused (hold). Continuing.`), runs the same prompt gate, and launches unfinished work before any new ticket (`unfinished: ...`). `/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` differ in one thing: stop also writes the completion report and records the end of the run.
- `/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` are the same teardown. Every row in `.warp/agents.json` is ended and marked halted, every Shuttle slot is freed, the state is pushed, and with `CURSOR_API_KEY` every cloud agent in the registry and every agent in this repo whose name carries this run's `[warp:<instance>]` tag has its run cancelled and is archived. Without the key the links are printed. The agent running the command and this run's parent are skipped. Nothing from before a halt is resumed: resume launches a new Shuttle on the same branch and counts no recovery. `/warp-cleanup` is the manual clear. `--running` also cancels agents that are still running and is refused while the run is running.
- Close the window, open a new one, `/warp-start`. State comes from main plus ticket folders. Every merge commits the live `.warp/beam.json`, journal, and board onto main. The ticket branch's beam is dropped. The end of each parent tick pushes that same beam. Tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs are stripped. `.warp/config.yaml` is not committed. `/warp-update-state` loads main, patches `.warp/tickets/<id>/` only, and puts back pause, stop, claims, and runState the parent has that main does not. `/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` run that sync before they return.
- A Shuttle writes `lastSeenAt` with `beam.py heartbeat`. The listener records `lastSeenAt` and `.warp/listener.json` with `inbound.py polled` at the end of its poll. `beam.py watchdog` runs on every tick and on start and resume. A fresh Shuttle heartbeat is left alone. Pause and stop do not start a replacement. The watchdog never starts a listener. The parent starts each poll from `orchestrator.py supervise`. A dead Shuttle becomes `recovering` on the same branch. Past `maxRecoveries` the alarm is `worker-died`. There is no process table. Plugin hooks do not run on cloud runners.
- `/warp-status`, `/warp-start`, and `/warp-resume` list every ticket that is not merged. Alarms and stalls lead, then `in progress N/maxInProgress`, or `holding new launches (N/maxInProgress), N fix workers running` when that cap is full, then `live shuttles N/cap` and the dead tickets waiting for a replacement, then a count of each state. Only a live Shuttle holds `maxAgents`. The same rows are `openWork` on the beam. `orchestrator.py supervise` marks a ticket stalled when its state makes no progress within that state's limit, logs it under `.warp/tickets/<id>/`, and tries one fix per stalled or alarmed ticket up to `maxStallFixes`. Past that cap the ticket parks and Slack gets one alarm. A digest posts every `statusDigestMinutes`. A new stall or alarm posts once. Every `repairSweepMinutes` (default 15) that pass also sweeps the whole run: what is broken, and what can be fixed that is not already queued or running. Broken covers tickets, pull requests, CI on main, locks, gates, the listener, and the beam, including red or stuck CI, a merge conflict, unresolved Bugbot findings, a stale or orphaned lock, a red base, a stuck gate, a listener poll that never reported, and a beam that is out of sync with main. The sweep reuses the fixer paths, starts work up to the slot cap, and logs findings and actions. `/warp-status` shows the last sweep. Slack is posted only when the sweep starts something new or escalates.
- The parent tick repairs `lock-escape` only, one ticket at a time, every `alarmRepairMinutes` (default 15). The repair widens that ticket's locks to the paths that escaped. It waits when an in-flight ticket holds one of those paths. Past `maxAlarmRepairs` (default 5) the alarm stays. `bugbot-failed`, `worker-died`, `stuck`, `ci-red`, and `gate-red` are left alone by `alarm_repair.py`. `orchestrator.py supervise` restarts a `worker-died` step, starts a fix Shuttle for `ci-red` and `bugbot-failed`, and restarts `stuck` up to `maxRecoveries`. `gate-red` stays on the gate. Pause and stop do not repair. The listener does not start the repair subagent. The parent starts one subagent in that ticket's worktree.
- A pending or stale-red gate clears when every member is merged or done and no check is actually red, then the tick runs. G1 with members P-002, P-003, P-004, and P-018 stores `members merged: P-002, P-003, P-004, P-018`. Herald posts `G1 pending cleared. Members merged. Tick ran.` A green gate stays green. Pause and stop do not recompute.
- `/warp-upgrade` replaces `.cursor/plugins/warp`. Run it in the repo that has Warp installed. When the source is a git checkout, it fetches the default branch. If the fetch fails, it prints `fetch failed` and `keeping the installed copy`. It does not overwrite `.warp/config.yaml` or the beam. It prints `Warp vX.Y.Z`. Reload Cursor, then confirm with `/warp-version`. `/warp-init` does not upgrade an existing copy. This command does. `--force` is not an upgrade flag. The flags are `--root` and `--source`.
 Init and upgrade write every `commands/*.md` file to `.cursor/commands/`, including `.cursor/commands/warp-upgrade.md`, so a reload lists `/warp-upgrade`, `/warp-update-state`, `/warp-status`, and the other commands when the plugin command index still omits that path.